<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mortimer Wheeler</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1890">1890</a>–<a href="/years/1976">1976</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Robert Eric Mortimer Wheeler</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Mortimer Wheeler was a British archaeologist and army officer who directed the National Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> Museum and in 1934 established the Institute of Archaeology in London. He argued for a scientific and methodical approach to excavation and the recording of stratigraphic context, developing the 'Wheeler method', and excavated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verulamium">Verulamium</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> hill fort of Maiden Castle. As director-general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archaeological-survey-of-india">Archaeological Survey of India</a> from 1944 he oversaw excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harappa">Harappa</a>, Arikamedu and Brahmagiri. His books and broadcasts brought archaeology to a mass audience, though many of his interpretations have since been discredited or reinterpreted.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mortimer-wheeler.jpg" alt="Mortimer Wheeler" width="330" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bassano Ltd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mortimer_Wheeler_in_1939.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Robert Eric Mortimer Wheeler was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> on 10 September 1890, the first child of the journalist Robert Mortimer Wheeler and his second wife Emily, and was raised largely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> before the family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. In 1907 he won a scholarship to read classical studies at University College London, where A. E. Housman taught him. He switched for a time to the Slade School of Fine Art but returned to classics and graduated with a second class degree.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he volunteered for the Royal Artillery, served on the Western Front, rose to major and was awarded the Military Cross. Afterwards he worked for the Royal Commission cataloguing the historic structures of Essex and published on Roman Colchester, and the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> awarded him a Doctorate of Letters for research on Romano-Rhenish pots.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Wheeler joined the National Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, first as Keeper of Archaeology and then as director, and with his first wife Tessa excavated the Roman forts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segontium">Segontium</a>, Y Gaer and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caerleon">Isca Augusta</a>. Influenced by Augustus Pitt Rivers, he argued that excavation and the recording of stratigraphic context needed a scientific and methodical approach, the Wheeler method. In July 1926 he became Keeper of the London Museum, where he reorganised the collection and began lecturing at University College London.</p><p>In 1934 he opened the Institute of Archaeology as part of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, as honorary director, hoping to professionalise the subject and make it, in his words, a laboratory of archaeological science. He oversaw excavations at Lydney Park, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verulamium">Verulamium</a> and Maiden Castle. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he rose to brigadier, serving in the North African Campaign and the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. In 1944 he became director-general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archaeological-survey-of-india">Archaeological Survey of India</a>, where excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harappa">Harappa</a>, Arikamedu and Brahmagiri took place and he reformed the establishment. His letters from that time contain racist remarks about Indians, and he beat some staff. After returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1948 he lectured and advised the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Knighted in 1952, Wheeler became a public figure through books, cruise ship lectures, radio and television, notably the BBC series Animal, Vegetable, Mineral?. He was president of the Society of Antiquaries from 1954 to 1959 and a Fellow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> in 1968. He died on 22 July 1976. Archaeologists Gabriel Moshenska and Tim Schadla-Hall have called him the best-known British archaeologist of the twentieth century. He is regarded as a major figure in establishing South Asian archaeology, though many of his interpretations of particular sites have been discredited or reinterpreted. Colleagues divided over him: some loved him and others despised him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mortimer Wheeler” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 1940s the British archaeologist <b>Mortimer Wheeler</b> blamed invading Aryans, pointing to skeletons found in the upper levels of Mohenjo-daro, but later study showed that they were not the victims of a single massacre, and the theory of a violent conquest has been abandoned.</blockquote><a href="/culture/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>At Brahmagiri in Karnataka, <b>Mortimer Wheeler</b>’s excavation of 1947 helped establish the culture sequence of south Indian prehistory.</blockquote><a href="/culture/south-indian-megalithic-culture">South Indian megalithic culture · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Leatherhead, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.86,-4.25&amp;year=1945" aria-label="Glasgow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4457 260 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mortimer Wheeler"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4907 530h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4907 530h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.86,-4.25&amp;year=1945">What was here in 1945? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
